    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Fractures and faults provide the primary control on the underground fluid flow through low permeability massive carbonate rocks. Fault cores often represent lower transmissibility whereas the surrounding damaged rocks and main slip surfaces are high transmissibility elements. We determined the physical properties of fault rocks collected in and around the fault cores of large normal faults in central Italy. Among the study samples, the cemented fault rocks have the lowest porosity and highest bulk and shear moduli. These values are quite similar to those of the Cretaceous, massive limestone host rock. Conversely, samples of both fault rocks and fractured limestones have higher porosity and lower bulk and shear moduli. These two porous rocks are however characterized by different pore geometries. The granular, not-cemented fault rock are made of a rigid pore frame, whereas the fractured limestone of soft pores. We calculate the permeability values for the granular not-cemented fault rock by using the Kozeny-Carman relation, and for the fractured host rocks by the Budianski-O’Connell relations for elastic moduli of cracked solids. For the granular, not-cemented fault rock results are consistent with values of permeability comprised between ~1.5*10⁻²  and 2*10⁰ mD. For the fractured host rock we compute values of permeability comprised between ~ 2*10 and 1.6*10²  mD.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
